---
// Robotics tab: six years of VEX, then relaunching Tri-County's FRC team.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro'
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro'
import TitleBlock from '../components/TitleBlock.astro'
import { site } from '../data/site'
import '../styles/pages/robotics.css'

import vexField from '../assets/photos/robotics/vex-robot-field.jpg'
import vexWorlds from '../assets/photos/robotics/vex-worlds.jpg'
import cad3236a from '../assets/photos/robotics/3236a-cad.jpg'
import build3236a from '../assets/photos/robotics/3236a-build.jpg'
import lebronBot from '../assets/photos/robotics/lebron-bot.jpg'
import frcShop from '../assets/photos/robotics/frc-shop.jpg'
import frcBoard from '../assets/photos/robotics/frc-whiteboard.jpg'

const facts = [
  { label: 'Running teams', value: 'Six years, 2021 to now' },
  { label: 'VEX World Championship', value: 'Twice, in middle school' },
  { label: 'High school VEX', value: 'Captain, team 3236A' },
  { label: 'Team size', value: '8 students' },
  { label: 'Code', value: 'C++ on EZ-Template' },
  { label: 'FRC', value: 'Co-founder, 2027 rookie team' },
  { label: 'FRC game', value: 'BIOCORE (2027)' },
  { label: 'School', value: site.schoolShort },
]

// VEX game names by season are public; Lucas's results per season are still todo.
const seasons = [
  { years: '2021 to 2022', game: 'Tipping Point', level: 'Middle school', note: 'First season. Team led to VEX Worlds.', todo: 'confirm this was a Worlds year, add team number and result' },
  { years: '2022 to 2023', game: 'Spin Up', level: 'Middle school', note: 'Back to VEX Worlds.', todo: 'confirm Worlds year, add team number and result' },
  { years: '2023 to 2024', game: 'Over Under', level: 'High school', note: '', todo: 'team, role, and how the season went' },
  { years: '2024 to 2025', game: 'High Stakes', level: 'High school', note: '', todo: 'team, role, and how the season went' },
  { years: '2025 to 2026', game: 'Push Back', level: 'High school', note: 'Captain of 3236A, a team of 8. Led design, code, and competition strategy.', todo: 'best finish, awards, any qualifier results' },
  { years: '2026 to 2027', game: 'FRC: BIOCORE', level: 'High school', note: 'Relaunching the school’s FRC team as a rookie.', todo: 'still doing VEX this year too? if so, add the game and team' },
]

const jsonLd = {
  '@type': 'Person',
  '@id': `${site.url}/#lucas`,
  memberOf: [
    {
      '@type': 'OrganizationRole',
      roleName: 'Team captain',
      memberOf: { '@type': 'SportsTeam', name: 'VEX Robotics team 3236A', sport: 'VEX Robotics Competition' },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'OrganizationRole',
      roleName: 'Co-founder and student leader',
      startDate: '2026',
      memberOf: { '@type': 'SportsTeam', name: 'Tri-County FRC', sport: 'FIRST Robotics Competition' },
    },
  ],
}
---
<Base
  title="Robotics"
  description="Six years running robotics teams: VEX Worlds twice in middle school, captain of VEX team 3236A, and relaunching Tri-County's FRC team from nothing for 2027."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <PageHeader
    title="Robotics"
    measure="2021 to now"
    lede="I've been running robotics teams for six years. It started with VEX in middle school, where my team made it to Worlds twice. Now I captain a high school VEX team and I'm helping restart my school's FRC team from zero. This page is how all of that actually works."
  />

  <section class="band-dark robo-hero" aria-label="VEX robot on the field">
    <div class="frame">
      <Figure
        src={vexField}
        alt="Team 3236A's VEX robot on the competition field during a Push Back match"
        caption="3236A on the field during the Push Back season."
        fig={1}
        bleed
        eager
        sizes="100vw"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="frame stack-md">
      <h2 class="visually-hidden">Robotics facts</h2>
      <TitleBlock items={facts} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- VEX: middle school -->
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="vex-start">
    <div class="frame split">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="vex-start">VEX, where it started</h2>
        <p>
          I joined VEX in 2021, in middle school, and ended up leading the team: the robot, the code,
          and who did what at competitions. <span class="todo">when you took over and how big the team was</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          We made it to the VEX World Championship twice. <span class="todo">years, team number, division, and how you placed at each Worlds</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          Worlds was the first time I saw how big this gets. Hundreds of teams, every one of them with
          a robot that solved the same game differently. <span class="todo">one specific memory from Worlds: a match, a robot you studied, something that went wrong</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          Middle school taught me the basics I still use. Get a robot driving early. Practice driving
          more than you think you need to. And the team that knows its own robot best usually beats
          the team with the fanciest one.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure
        src={vexWorlds}
        alt="Lucas's middle school VEX team with their robot at the VEX World Championship"
        caption="At VEX Worlds with my middle school team."
        fig={2}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- VEX: high school captain -->
  <section class="section band-dark robo-band" aria-labelledby="vex-captain">
    <div class="frame stack-lg">
      <div class="column prose">
        <h2 id="vex-captain">Captain of 3236A</h2>
        <p>
          In high school I captain VEX team 3236A. There are 8 of us. For the Push Back season I led
          the design process, the code, and our competition strategy, and I ran the team at events.
        </p>
        <p>
          Eight people is a strange size. It's too many for everyone to work on the robot at once, and
          too few to split into real subteams. Most of my job as captain is making sure nobody is
          standing around and nobody is stuck doing the same thing all season.
          <span class="todo">how you actually split the 8 (builders, coders, driver, notebook?)</span>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="robo-pair">
        <Figure
          src={cad3236a}
          alt="CAD model of team 3236A's Push Back robot"
          caption="The 3236A robot in CAD before we cut anything."
          fig={3}
        />
        <Figure
          src={build3236a}
          alt="Team 3236A's robot partly built on a workbench"
          caption="Mid-build on the bench."
          fig={4}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="robo-how">
        <div class="robo-how-item">
          <h3>Design process</h3>
          <p>
            Every season starts with the game manual. Before anyone builds, we figure out how matches
            are scored and what a winning robot has to do. Then we sketch options, pick one, and get a
            drivetrain moving as fast as possible so the drivers can start practicing.
          </p>
          <p class="small muted"><span class="todo">how 3236A's Push Back robot scores, and one design you threw out and why</span></p>
        </div>
        <div class="robo-how-item">
          <h3>Code</h3>
          <p>
            I write our code in C++ on EZ-Template, a library for VEX V5 that handles the drive PID and
            the autonomous selector. That frees me to spend my time on routes and mechanisms instead of
            rewriting the same drive math every season.
          </p>
          <p class="small muted"><span class="todo">sensors you use, how many autons you keep, how you tune them</span></p>
        </div>
        <div class="robo-how-item">
          <h3>Competition strategy</h3>
          <p>
            At events I handle strategy: which auton to run against which alliance, what to tell our
            partner before a match, and what to change between matches when something breaks.
          </p>
          <p class="small muted"><span class="todo">do you drive? who scouts, and how (paper, spreadsheet, app)?</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Season list -->
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="seasons">
    <div class="frame stack-md">
      <h2 id="seasons">Season by season</h2>
      <ul class="ruled-list season-list">
        {seasons.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <p class="season-years">{s.years}</p>
            <div>
              <h3>{s.game}</h3>
              <p class="small muted">{s.level}</p>
            </div>
            <p>
              {s.note && <span>{s.note} </span>}
              <span class="todo">{s.todo}</span>
            </p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- A season, in order -->
  <section class="section band-tint" aria-labelledby="season-shape">
    <div class="frame stack-md">
      <div class="column prose">
        <h2 id="season-shape">What a season looks like</h2>
        <p>Every VEX season runs on roughly the same loop. The game changes, the order doesn't.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="season-steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Game reveal</h3>
          <p>Read the manual as a team. Work out how points are scored and what wins matches.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Ideas on the board</h3>
          <p>Everyone sketches. We compare designs against the scoring, not against who drew them.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>CAD and first build</h3>
          <p>Model the robot, then build a driving base early so practice can start.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Code and autons</h3>
          <p>Drive code first, then autonomous routes on EZ-Template, tuned on a practice field.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Compete and rebuild</h3>
          <p>Every event shows what's weak. Fix it before the next one. Most robots get rebuilt mid-season.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Qualify or regroup</h3>
          <p>Push for States and Worlds. If we don't make it, write down why before next season.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="small muted column"><span class="todo">check this matches how your seasons really go; add rough months for each step</span></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Lessons -->
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lessons">
    <div class="frame column prose">
      <h2 id="lessons">What leading my friends taught me</h2>
      <p>
        The hardest part of captaining isn't the robot. It's that everyone on the team is my age, and
        a lot of them are my friends. I can't order anyone around, and I wouldn't want to.
      </p>
      <p>
        What works is making decisions out loud with reasons. If I say "we're switching intakes," I
        need to show why the old one loses us points. When people see the reasoning, they argue with
        the reasoning instead of with me, and that's a much better argument to have.
      </p>
      <p>
        I've also learned to hand off things I'd rather do myself. If I write all the code and drive
        every match, the team falls apart the year I leave. Teaching someone else is slower this
        season and faster every season after.
      </p>
      <p><span class="todo">one real moment: a disagreement on the team and how it got settled</span></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FRC -->
  <section class="band-dark robo-hero" aria-label="The LeBron bot">
    <div class="frame">
      <Figure
        src={lebronBot}
        alt="The LeBron bot, a basketball-shooting robot built on a tank-drive chassis"
        caption="The LeBron bot: a basketball shooter on a tank chassis, our warm-up build before the real season."
        fig={5}
        bleed
        sizes="100vw"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="frc">
    <div class="frame stack-lg">
      <div class="column prose">
        <h2 id="frc">Restarting FRC at Tri-County</h2>
        <p>
          In 2026, two other student leaders and I started rebuilding our school's FRC team for the
          2027 season. The game is BIOCORE. We started with no robot and no budget.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tri-County had an FRC team years ago that went dormant in 2019. We aren't picking up where it
          left off. We're registering as a brand-new rookie team, so its history isn't ours to claim.
          Everything on this page is what we've built since starting over.
        </p>

        <h3>Starting from nothing</h3>
        <p>
          FRC is a lot bigger than VEX. The robots are full size, the parts are real metal, and just
          registering costs real money. With no budget, the first job wasn't the robot at all. It was
          finding people willing to fund one.
        </p>
        <p>
          So I cold-emailed local companies, explained who we are, and asked them to sponsor a rookie
          team. Writing an email a stranger actually reads is its own skill. <span class="todo">roughly how many companies you emailed and how many replied</span>
        </p>

        <h3>The LeBron bot</h3>
        <p>
          Before the real season we needed something to build, mostly to learn how to work together
          and what FRC parts feel like. We built the LeBron bot: a basketball shooter on a tank chassis.
          It's a goofy name for a serious test of our drivetrain, our wiring, and our programming.
        </p>
        <p><span class="todo">how it shoots (flywheel? catapult?), how far, and what broke first</span></p>
      </div>

      <div class="split robo-rules">
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>Two team rules</h3>
          <p>We set two rules early, and they've done more for the team than any single design.</p>
          <ul class="ruled-list rules-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Ideas go on the board with no names.</strong> Nobody's idea wins because it's
              theirs, and nobody's gets ignored because they're new. We judge the sketch, not the person.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>If it can be tested in under 10 minutes, we test it.</strong> We don't spend half an
              hour debating something we could just try. Build the quick version, run it, and the result
              settles it.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <Figure
          src={frcBoard}
          alt="A whiteboard covered in unlabeled robot sketches from an FRC design meeting"
          caption="Ideas on the board, no names attached."
          fig={6}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band-dark robo-band" aria-labelledby="frc-votech">
    <div class="frame stack-lg">
      <Figure
        src={frcShop}
        alt="Tri-County FRC students working together in the school shop"
        caption="The team working in the shop."
        fig={7}
        bleed
        sizes="100vw"
      />
      <p class="small muted"><span class="todo">Fig. 7 caption: add teammates' first names, left to right</span></p>
      <div class="split">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 id="frc-votech">Why FRC fits a vocational school</h2>
          <p>
            At a lot of schools, FRC is the only place students touch a mill or crimp a wire. At
            Tri-County, students already learn those things in their shop programs. FRC gives
            them a reason to use all of it on one machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            That's the pitch. Students run the whole thing: design, machining, wiring, and programming.
            The robot gets made by the people who are training to do this for a living.
          </p>
          <p><span class="todo">which shops your teammates come from, and the machines you can use</span></p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <h2>The team website</h2>
          <p>
            I built the team's website by hand in plain HTML and CSS, no framework. It exists mainly so
            sponsors who get my emails can see who we are and what their money would do.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/projects/frc-website/">How I built the FRC team website</a>
          </p>
          <p class="small muted"><span class="todo">add the live site link once it's public</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="next">
    <div class="frame column prose">
      <h2 id="next">What's next</h2>
      <p>
        Kickoff for BIOCORE is in January 2027. Between now and then the job is the same as it's been
        since we started: get sponsors to commit, get new students comfortable in the shop, and make
        sure the team can run without any one of the three of us.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you're a local company that might sponsor a rookie FRC team, or you just want to talk
        robotics, email me at <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/projects/">See my projects</a>
        <a class="button button-quiet" href="/experience/">Work experience</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
